menu "WellD Sensor"

    config WELLD_SENSOR_SHUNT_MILLIOHMS
        int "Shunt resistor value (milliohms)"
        default 100000
        range 1000 130000
        help
            Value in milliohms. 100000 = 100 Ω (PCB R2, 0.1 %) → 4 mA gives
            0.40 V, 20 mA gives 2.00 V at ADS1115 AIN0 (read on the ±4.096 V
            range). Upper bound 130 Ω keeps 21 mA — the short-circuit
            threshold — at 2.73 V, inside the ADS1115 input range even when
            the 3.3 V rail sags to ~2.9 V in HT7333 dropout near battery-empty
            (analog inputs cannot be measured above VDD).

    config WELLD_SENSOR_MAX_DEPTH_CM
        int "Maximum well depth (centimeters)"
        default 600
        help
            Depth at 20 mA (full-scale output of the transducer). 600 = 6 m.

    config WELLD_SENSOR_OFFSET_CM
        int "Water level offset (centimeters, signed)"
        default 0
        range -600 600
        help
            Added to the computed level after scaling. Use a negative value
            when the transducer sits below the true zero reference point
            (e.g. -50 if it hangs 50 cm below the water outlet pipe).
            Can be overridden at runtime via NVS key "welld/offset_cm".
            Both this default and any runtime value written to NVS key
            'welld/offset_cm' are clamped to the range -600 to +600 cm at
            runtime.

    config WELLD_BATT_REPORT_ENABLED
        bool "Report battery voltage over Zigbee (EP2)"
        default y
        help
            Registers Zigbee endpoint 2 (Analog Input, battery volts) and
            reports the battery voltage to the coordinator on every wakeup.
            The battery is always measured internally via the ADS1115 AIN2
            gated resistor divider for the low-battery guard, regardless of
            this option — disabling it only removes the Zigbee endpoint.

    config WELLD_BATT_DIVIDER_RATIO
        int "Battery divider ratio * 100 (e.g. 200 = 2:1 divider)"
        default 200
        range 100 1000
        help
            PCB R7/R8 = 100 kΩ / 100 kΩ divider = ratio 200 (1S board, 2026-07-19);
            VBAT range 3.0–4.2 V. Reported voltage = ADC voltage * ratio / 100.
            The AIN2 read uses the ADS1115 ±4.096 V PGA, so the 2.1 V divider
            maximum at full charge fits with margin (±2.048 V would clip).
            Used for the ADS1115 AIN2 battery divider read (the only battery
            measurement path on this hardware).

    config WELLD_BATT_FULL_MV
        int "Battery full voltage (mV)"
        default 4200
        range 3000 12000
        help
            Voltage read at 100 % state of charge. 4200 = 1S 18650 (4.2 V).
            Used by the Z2M converter to compute the battery percentage.
            (The low-battery send/NVS-skip guard uses WELLD_BATT_EMPTY_MV,
            not this option.)

    config WELLD_BATT_EMPTY_MV
        int "Battery empty voltage (mV)"
        default 3200
        range 2000 10000
        help
            Voltage read at 0 % state of charge. When the battery (resting,
            read before the radio) is at or below this threshold the device
            skips the Zigbee send and all NVS writes and sleeps for the
            maximum interval to conserve charge.
            3200 keeps the ESP32-C6 in spec: below ~3.4 V the HT7333-A LDO is
            in dropout, so the 3.3 V rail is VBAT minus ~60-100 mV of dropout
            and the cell/protection-FET sag under the 802.15.4 TX burst. At a
            3.0 V resting cell that rail is ~2.9 V — under the C6's 3.0 V
            minimum. The cost is the last ~5 % of an 18650's capacity.

    config WELLD_DS18B20_GPIO
        int "GPIO number for DS18B20 data line"
        default 7
        range 0 21
        help
            GPIO connected to the DS18B20 data pin via a 4.7 kΩ pull-up to 3.3 V.
            External power mode is required: connect DS18B20 VCC to 3.3 V and GND
            to GND. Parasite-power mode (VCC pin tied to GND) is not supported —
            the RMT one-wire driver does not drive a strong pull-up during
            conversion, so a parasite-powered sensor will stall and return an
            out-of-range reading that is silently discarded every wakeup.

    config WELLD_I2C_SDA_GPIO
        int "I2C SDA GPIO (ADS1115)"
        range 0 23
        default 18
        help
            GPIO number for the I2C SDA line. Used by ADS1115 (16-bit ADC,
            0x48). GPIO18 on the WellD PCB: GPIO10/11 are NOT bonded out on the
            ESP32-C6-MINI-1 module, so I2C moved to GPIO18/19 (2026-07-21).
            MAX17048 removed in an earlier hardware redesign.

    config WELLD_I2C_SCL_GPIO
        int "I2C SCL GPIO (ADS1115)"
        range 0 23
        default 19
        help
            GPIO number for the I2C SCL line. GPIO19 on the WellD PCB (see
            WELLD_I2C_SDA_GPIO — GPIO10/11 are unavailable on the MINI-1 module).

    config WELLD_VLOOP_GPIO
        int "VLOOP 16 V boost enable GPIO (MT3608B EN)"
        range 0 21
        default 5
        help
            Driven HIGH before each 4-20 mA transducer read and held for
            WELLD_VLOOP_SETTLE_MS, then LOW immediately after the conversion.

    config WELLD_VLOOP_SETTLE_MS
        int "Loop warm-up before the 4-20 mA reading (ms)"
        range 10 1000
        default 10
        help
            Time VLOOP is held on before the shunt is sampled. It must cover
            both the 16 V rail and the transducer: the boost output caps
            (C20/C22) charge through the Q3 load-disconnect P-FET, so the rail
            needs longer than the bare MT3608B soft-start (< 5 ms typical) -
            10 ms is the PCB minimum. The transducer itself must also have
            reached a valid output by then; some need tens or hundreds of
            milliseconds after power-up, so set this to the start-up
            ("switch-on") time in its datasheet if that is longer. Each extra
            millisecond keeps the boost and a 4-20 mA load on: roughly
            15-75 mA from the cell per wakeup while it lasts. Above 10 ms the
            firmware samples AIN0 once the rail is up and switches the loop
            straight off if it is pinned at a dead short (>= 3.0 V), so a
            shorted cable is not held at the boost's current limit for the
            whole warm-up.

    config WELLD_BATT_DIV_EN_GPIO
        int "Battery divider enable GPIO (Q2 gate)"
        range 0 21
        default 15
        help
            Drives Q2 gate (AO3400A). HIGH enables the R7/R8 resistor divider
            (100 kΩ / 100 kΩ on the 1S board) connected to ADS1115 AIN2 via
            the Q5 high-side P-FET. Drive HIGH for 1 ms
            before reading; LOW after to prevent quiescent divider drain.

    config WELLD_ADS1115_DRDY_GPIO
        int "ADS1115 ALERT/DRDY GPIO"
        range 0 23
        default 22
        help
            GPIO connected to the ADS1115 ALERT/DRDY open-drain output.
            GPIO22 on the WellD PCB — moved off GPIO12 (2026-07-22) so the
            native USB D− pin can serve the USB-C console/programming path.
            The sensor component puts the ADS1115 in conversion-ready mode
            (Hi_thresh = 0x8000, Lo_thresh = 0x0000: Hi MSB = 1, Lo MSB = 0
            per the datasheet) so the pin asserts LOW when each single-shot
            conversion completes. The GPIO is an input with the internal
            pull-up enabled (ALERT/DRDY is open-drain; the PCB adds R_DRDY
            4.7 kΩ). A falling-edge ISR releases a binary semaphore so the
            sensor task can block instead of polling; the conversion is then
            confirmed via the config register's OS bit, and a missing edge
            falls back to polling after 10 ms.

    config WELLD_SOLAR_DETECT_GPIO
        int "Solar charging detect GPIO (/CHRG, active-low)"
        range 0 21
        default 6
        help
            Input. Active-low: LOW means the CN3791 solar MPPT charger is
            actively charging. HIGH means no solar charging. Reported to the
            Zigbee coordinator as part of the status payload.

    config WELLD_ADC_OVERSAMPLE_ENABLED
        bool "ADC oversampling (median-of-three)"
        default n
        help
            Take 3 ADS1115 samples per channel and report the median.
            Rejects single-sample noise outliers at the cost of ~3 ms extra
            active time per channel (two more ~1.2 ms conversions plus I2C).
            Enable for electrically noisy installations; leave off for
            maximum battery life.

    config WELLD_TEMP_COMPENSATION_ENABLED
        bool "Temperature compensation for water level"
        default y
        help
            Compensate water-level readings for the change of water density
            with temperature. A transducer's metre scale is a pressure that
            assumes water of one density (see WELLD_TEMP_COMPENSATION_REF_C);
            water at another temperature is less dense, so a given column
            presses less and reads low. The correction is small in well
            water (0.03 % at 10 °C, 0.07 % at 14 °C for the 4 °C reference)
            and grows in warm water (0.43 % at 30 °C). Skipped when the
            DS18B20 read fails (guard in main.c: temp > -127.0 °C) and
            outside 0-40 °C.

    config WELLD_TEMP_COMPENSATION_REF_C
        int "Water temperature the transducer's metre scale assumes (°C)"
        default 4
        range 0 40
        depends on WELLD_TEMP_COMPENSATION_ENABLED
        help
            A transducer's "0-5 m" range is really a pressure range. Nearly
            every datasheet uses the conventional metre of water column
            (9.80665 kPa, i.e. 1000 kg/m3 - water at its densest, about
            4 °C): leave this at 4. Change it only if your transducer states
            a different reference, e.g. 20 for "calibrated in 20 °C water".
            The level is multiplied by rho(ref)/rho(T), using the Tanaka
            (2001) density of pure water.

    config WELLD_DS18B20_RESOLUTION_BITS
        int "DS18B20 resolution (bits)"
        default 11
        range 9 12
        help
            Conversion wait per wakeup (the ds18b20 driver's timing, just
            above the datasheet maximums): 9-bit = 100 ms, 10-bit = 200 ms,
            11-bit = 400 ms, 12-bit = 800 ms. Lower resolution saves active
            time and battery. 11-bit gives 0.125 °C steps — adequate for well
            water monitoring — and saves 400 ms per wakeup compared to 12-bit.

endmenu
